  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Export Options**\n\nI\'m currently evaluating the best methods for exporting interactive canvas elements. Specifically, I\'m leaning toward native SVG-based rendering within the canvas, or potentially a combined Canvas 2D / SVG renderer. I find the SVG canvas approach attractive, especially because each element, such as a land boundary, becomes an individual, manipulable SVG object.\n\n\n**Evaluating Export Formats**\n\nI\'ve been exploring direct SVG export as a promising approach. Serializing the SVG DOM to a `.svg` file seems lossless and precise. High-resolution PNG exports are also under consideration; these could be generated by rendering the SVG on a scaled-up HTML5 canvas. The goal is to provide crisp raster outputs for printing and sharing. I\'m prioritizing SVG\'s interactive manipulation capabilities, for smooth element dragging, resizing, and snapping. It all leads to avoiding canvas library bundling issues.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 2
      nanos: 29571490
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
